@startuml sc01-greenfield-allocation
title Scenario uc02 — greenfield identity allocation\nA designer asks for a valve and is given both an identity and a code

skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 220
skinparam ParticipantPadding 12
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Designer" as DesignerUser

box "SimHost — simulated participants"
  participant "ENG" as ENG
  participant "REG-LOCATION" as LOC
  participant "MMS" as MMS
end box

participant "Identity service" as IDS
participant "ws-ISBM provider" as ISBM
participant "ws-CIR provider" as CIR

note over DesignerUser, IDS
  uc01 authors TIC-106 in the scenario file, so only
  the identity is minted. Here the **code is allocated
  too**, and the scenario deliberately does not know
  it in advance.
end note

== Allocation ==

DesignerUser -> ENG : "I need a valve"\ncodePrefix P-, no number
activate ENG

ENG -> IDS : allocate(codePrefix = "P-")
activate IDS
IDS -> IDS : read high-water mark\nfor the P- series
IDS -> IDS : mint FederationId\nassign CodeSequence = max + 1
IDS --> ENG : FederationId + code
deactivate IDS

note right of IDS
  Two separate things, and the distinction is
  the point of the scenario. The code is a
  **name** — local, sequential, reissuable in
  principle. The FederationId is the **identity**
  — minted once and never again.

  A CodeAssignment without CodePrefix and
  CodeSequence would still yield a valid-looking
  code, but the next allocation could not know
  where the series got to.
end note

ENG -> ENG : classify against\nrdl:TemperatureIndicatingController
ENG -> ENG : persist Tag + CodeAssignment\n+ Control action = Reverse
ENG --> DesignerUser : P-00n allocated
deactivate ENG

== A second request continues the series ==

DesignerUser -> ENG : "another valve"
activate ENG
ENG -> IDS : allocate(codePrefix = "P-")
activate IDS
IDS --> ENG : new FederationId, CodeSequence + 1
deactivate IDS
ENG --> DesignerUser : P-00(n+1)
deactivate ENG

note over ENG
  The assertions check relationships, never
  literals: "the newest allocation", "one greater
  than the previous", "no two rows share a
  sequence". Those hold on the first run and the
  fiftieth — so the scenario tests the allocator
  rather than the reset.
end note

== Registration and dispatch ==

ENG -> ISBM : PostRequest(ProcessRegistry)
activate ISBM
ISBM -> CIR : deliver
activate CIR
CIR -> CIR : register SourceID=ENG\nassign CIRIDs
CIR -> ISBM : AcknowledgeRegistry
deactivate CIR
ISBM --> ENG : AcknowledgeRegistry
deactivate ISBM

ENG -> ISBM : PostPublication(SyncSegments)\non /Enterprise/Site/Eng

== The identity travels unchanged ==

ISBM -> LOC : ReadPublication → SyncSegments
activate LOC
LOC -> LOC : leaf class unknown\nbind at rdl:Instrument (degraded)
LOC -> LOC : adopt FederationId\nassign its own location code
LOC -> LOC : ingest Control action as **mapped**\n— the definition is held, the class is not
deactivate LOC

LOC -> ISBM : PostPublication(SyncSegments)\non /Enterprise/Site/OandM

ISBM -> MMS : ReadPublication → SyncSegments
activate MMS
MMS -> ISBM : PostRequest(GetRegistry)
activate ISBM
ISBM -> CIR : deliver
activate CIR
CIR -> ISBM : ShowRegistry (CIRID cluster)
deactivate CIR
ISBM --> MMS : ShowRegistry
deactivate ISBM
MMS -> MMS : adopt FederationId\nassign EquipmentNumber
MMS -> MMS : retain all values **unmapped**\n— no reference data at all
deactivate MMS

note over ENG, MMS
  Three participants, three codes, **one identity
  and one minter**. Adopting an identity is not
  minting one, and a second mint here would be
  the failure the model exists to prevent — the
  assertions check for exactly that.
end note

@enduml
